/// Reading a document in one Resource Description Framework (RDF) syntax.
///
/// A parser knows its syntax and nothing more than the syntax requires. It reads bytes and
/// writes the statements they make, in order and as written. For syntaxes in the Turtle
/// family it leaves Internationalized Resource Identifier (IRI) resolution, prefix
/// expansion, blank node identity and term checking to the host. A syntax that scopes its
/// base or its prefixes by position in a tree, as JSON-LD and RDF/XML do, resolves those
/// itself and writes absolute IRIs, because a flat sequence of declarations cannot express
/// the scoping.
interface parser {
  use syntax.{syntax-description};
  use terms.{located-statement, span};

  /// The host's information about a document, given to a parser before it reads it.
  record parse-options {
    /// The IRI the document was retrieved from, or the base IRI the host chooses for it.
    /// A parser that writes relative references may ignore it, because the host resolves
    /// them against the same base. A parser that resolves IRIs itself uses it.
    base: option<string>,
    /// The media type the document arrived with, including any parameters, such as a
    /// JSON-LD profile.
    media-type: option<string>,
  }

  /// A part of the document that is not valid in the syntax.
  record syntax-error {
    /// A description of the fault, in English.
    message: string,
    /// The bytes at fault.
    span: span,
  }

  /// The reason a parse ended before the end of the document.
  variant parse-error {
    /// The document is not valid in the syntax.
    syntax(syntax-error),
    /// The document uses a feature of the syntax that this parser does not implement. The
    /// string names it in English.
    unsupported(string),
    /// Any other failure, with a description in English when the parser has one.
    other(option<string>),
  }

  /// Describes the syntax this parser reads.
  describe: func() -> syntax-description;

  /// Reads the document arriving on `document`, and writes the statements it makes to the
  /// returned stream.
  ///
  /// The document arrives as bytes in its own encoding; detecting the encoding is part of
  /// the syntax. Statements are written as soon as they are complete. The returned future
  /// resolves once the document has been read to its end, or with the first error, after
  /// which no further statements are written. Statements written before an error stay
  /// written, and the host decides whether to keep them.
  ///
  /// If the host drops the statement stream, the parser stops reading the document, and
  /// the host need not read the future.
  ///
  /// The function is asynchronous because the parser returns its stream and future before
  /// it has read the document, and goes on writing to them afterwards. Only an
  /// asynchronous export has a task that outlives its return, in which that work can run.
  parse: async func(
    document: stream<u8>,
    options: parse-options,
  ) -> tuple<stream<located-statement>, future<result<_, parse-error>>>;
}

/// A component that reads one RDF syntax.
///
/// The world imports no functions. Its component still imports the `syntax` and `terms`
/// interfaces, because the component model imports every interface whose types an export
/// uses, but those interfaces declare types only, so there is nothing in them to call. The
/// document and everything else the parser needs arrive as arguments.
world parser-component {
  export parser;
}
